👉 String Memory in Java -> Java stores String objects in a special memory area called the String Pool to improve memory efficiency.
                             The String Pool is a special area in the Heap Memory where Java stores unique string literals.

              Heap Memory
+---------------------------------------+
|                                       |
|      String Pool (Special Area)       |
|                                       |
|      "Java"                           |
|      "Python"                         |
|      "Hello"                          |
|                                       |
+---------------------------------------+

👉 == Operator -> The == operator compares object references (memory addresses), not the contents.

    String s1 = "Java";
    String s2 = "Java";
    System.out.println(s1 == s2);   // True

👉 equals() -> The equals() method compares the contents of two strings.

    String s1 = new String("Java");
    String s2 = new String("Java");
    System.out.println(s1.equals(s2));

👉 intern() Method -> The intern() method returns a reference from the String Pool.

    String s1 = new String("Java");
    String s2 = s1.intern();
    String s3 = "Java";
    System.out.println(s2 == s3);

Mutable vs Immutable
__________________________________________________
| Feature            | Mutable       | Immutable |
| ------------------ | ------------- | --------- |
| Can Change         | Yes           | No        | 
| New Object Created | No            | Yes       |
| Example            | StringBuilder | String    |
--------------------------------------------------

=================
| StringBuilder |
=================
👉 StringBuilder is a mutable sequence of characters used to create and modify strings efficiently.
👉 StringBuilder is faster then String b'coz StringBuilder modifies the existing object instead of creating a new object every time.
👉 String is Immutable

👉 append() -> The append() method adds data at the end of the existing sequence.

    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Java");
    sb.append(" Programming");
    System.out.println(sb);

👉 length() -> The length() method returns the number of characters currently stored in the StringBuilder.

    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Programming");
    System.out.println(sb.length());

👉 capacity() -> The capacity() method returns the current storage capacity of the StringBuilder.

    Length = Current characters
    Capacity = Maximum characters that can be stored before resizing

    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    System.out.println(sb.capacity());

👉 toString() -> The toString() method converts a StringBuilder object into a String object.

    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Java");
    String s = sb.toString();
    System.out.println(s);


